-The chapters can be read in sequence as a course, or selectively: Chapters~\ref{sec:Principles} and~\ref{sec:classicalSF} form a self-contained introduction to estimation-based fusion, Chapters~\ref{sec:learning} and~\ref{sec:application} a self-contained introduction to learning-based perception and its deployment, and Chapters~\ref{sec:MAS} to~\ref{sec:open-challenges} are intended for readers who already know one of the two traditions and want to see how the other completes it. Each technical chapter ends with problems that mix derivations, design questions, and small computations; model solutions are collected at the end of the book. The computational problems are supported by the companion repository, which contains reproducible Python examples organised chapter by chapter (\repopath{companion\_\allowbreak repo/\allowbreak examples} and \repopath{companion\_\allowbreak repo/\allowbreak exercises/\allowbreak chNN}), the scripts that generate the data behind the figures, and a notebook version of every script (\repopath{*.ipynb}) so that the examples can be run and modified interactively; the data files needed to compile the book are shipped with it, so that the manuscript does not depend on the repository, but every number and figure can be regenerated from it. We recommend running the examples alongside the text, modifying the noise models, reliability weights, and timestamp offsets, and observing how the consistency checks of the classical layer react; this is the most direct way to see how the description of uncertainty drives the behaviour of a fusion system.
